A34 — hold the fps cap to the target frame time, and let -r be the grid alone

Two changes to the session's own pacing, one per path.

The fps cap. `steamcompmgr_should_vblank_window` drops callbacks on a
`vblank_idx % (refresh / target)` grid whenever a fixed-rate panel is in use.
That grid is unrelated to when the guest committed, so a title already at its
own ceiling loses a whole cycle whenever its commit lands late or off the grid.
The VRR arm instead defers on `w->last_commit_first_latch_time + cycle`, a lower
bound anchored to the frame's own latch. The fixed-rate arm takes the same
deferral, and the limiter's cycle becomes the target frame time on both arms
(`mHzToRefreshCycle(ConvertHztomHz(target))`, exactly `1/target`). An integer
number of panel cycles quantises the cap and collapses to a single cycle for a
target that does not divide the advertised rate (60 on 90Hz), i.e. no cap at
all; the deferred lower bound cannot fall below the guest's own rate, so the cap
is exact where it bites and absent where the guest is already slower.

The `-r` mode lookup. `-r` is the session's vblank grid, while the panel's real
rate is what the DRM mode advertises. `init_drm` hands the grid to
`preferred_refresh`, and the mode search requires `mode->vrefresh` to equal it,
so a grid that matches no mode would otherwise fall through to the first mode on
the connector. The lookup now re-finds by the requested size with the refresh
unconstrained, so the nested refresh is free to differ from the mode's rate and
a connector whose modes all carry the real rate still yields an output.

